  "sections": [
    {
      "key": "quickFacts",
      "source": "glaser",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.aquariumglaser.de/en/17-rainbow-fishes-and-co/melanotaenia_pygmaea_en/",
      "html": "<p>The specific name \"pygmaea\" refers to a small body size. The fish obtained were fully coloured and about 4 cm long (total length). According to literature the maximum length in males is about 7 cm, whereas females always stay smaller and hardly ever reach 5 cm.</p>"
    },
    {
      "key": "distribution",
      "source": "glaser",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.aquariumglaser.de/en/17-rainbow-fishes-and-co/melanotaenia_pygmaea_en/",
      "html": "<p>Melanotaenia pygmaea is known from a very small area only. The species is endemic, meaning it lives only there, in tributaries of the Prince Regent River in the Kimberley region of the state of Western Australia. The aquarium strains most probably go back to collections made in 1992 and 1994. The species was discovered in 1974 and formally described in 1978.</p>"
    },
    {
      "key": "reproduction",
      "source": "glaser",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.aquariumglaser.de/en/17-rainbow-fishes-and-co/melanotaenia_pygmaea_en/",
      "html": "<p>The fish were obtained from a breeder (captive bred).</p>"
    }
  ],
